---
/**
 * ONE control for "which platforms am I looking at", on every surface that asks it.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS. Dan, 2026-08-20: "My concern is what if we have more than 3 — this layout starts
 * to break. How would a multiselect dropdown work?" He is right, and it was not one break but four.
 * The same question was drawn four ways, each with its own class family, its own grayscale rule and
 * its own pressed state: `.pf-chip` in the docs sidebar, `.sm-plat-chip` in the search modal,
 * `.cd-plat-chip` in the chat, `.rb-seg` on the roadmap. At six platforms all four fail separately,
 * and three of them were already failing at three: the sidebar has roughly 190px and one chip is a
 * 13px mark plus a 6px gap plus a 12px name plus 20px of padding, so "Airtable" alone is 88px and
 * the row has wrapped to two lines since the day it shipped. That wrap is the thing Dan has now
 * asked about twice.
 *
 * ONE SEMANTICS, ONE PRESENTATION, AND BOTH SECOND ANSWERS WERE DELETED ON 2026-08-21.
 *
 * This control shipped with two behaviours (`narrow` and `scope`) and two presentations (a row of
 * inline chips, and a disclosure over checkboxes). Oleh, 2026-08-21, looking at the board: now that
 * the documentation covers two platforms we always use the pattern with this dropdown and select,
 * because it works more flexibly — it lets you turn them all on, turn them all off, and we do not
 * need two extra states; for the user it is as clear as it can be. We apply that pattern
 * everywhere.
 *
 * So: the disclosure is the whole control. `chipsUpTo`, the `collapsed` comparison and the entire
 * `.pk-chip` family are gone, and with them the argument they carried — that a build-time count
 * could stand in for a measurement of how wide a NAME is. What that argument bought was a slightly
 * airier control on the two widest surfaces; what it cost was a reader meeting two different
 * shapes of one question depending on which surface they were standing on, and four per-surface
 * numbers that had to be re-measured every time a layout moved. A reader learns this control once.
 *
 * THE ONLY THING THAT VARIES NOW IS SIZE, and it varies on one axis with two values — see `size`.
 *
 * ZERO TICKED IS NO FILTER, AND THAT REPLACED A LOCK. Until this pass the last surviving platform
 * refused to switch off ("One platform always stays on."), because an empty set meant a manual
 * filtered to nothing. It now means the opposite, which is what a checkbox filter means everywhere
 * else: nothing ticked is nothing narrowed, so everything shows. That is Oleh's "turn them all on,
 * turn them all off" and it DELETES a special case instead of explaining one — the lock needed an
 * inert row, a hint sentence, an `aria-disabled` state and a guard in the commit path, four pieces
 * of machinery to describe a state the reader is now simply allowed to be in. `All` and `None`
 * buttons put both ends one click away.
 *
 * The trigger says `All platforms` at both ends, because both ends ARE the same view. What the
 * ticks preserve is the reader's own position: someone who pressed `None` finds their boxes empty
 * when they come back, and someone who has never touched the control finds them full. Nothing on
 * the site filters differently between those two.
 *
 * WHERE THE ANSWER LIVES IS A SEPARATE QUESTION FROM WHAT THE ANSWER MEANS, and it is its own
 * prop. `state="shared"` reads and writes the one stored preference, so setting it in the sidebar
 * repaints the search modal and the chat live. `state="local"` keeps the set on this element and
 * announces it with `pk:change`. The roadmap is local; the argument is in `lib/platform-picker.ts`.
 *
 * WHAT THE ROADMAP GAINED AND WHAT IT GAVE UP is argued in `pages/roadmap.astro` where the old
 * control's reasoning lived, and in `lib/board.ts` at the item loop. The short version: an untagged
 * request is one that applies to EVERY platform, so hiding it while filtering to Airtable dropped
 * precisely the rows an Airtable user most needed to see.
 *
 * THE COLLAPSED STATE IS LEGIBLE WITHOUT OPENING IT. A trigger reading "Platforms" tells a reader
 * nothing about what is being kept from them, and this control is only ever interesting when it is
 * narrowed. So the trigger carries the marks of what is ON plus a count, and falls back to a plain
 * "All platforms" at rest. That is the ONLY statement of the filter's state on the sidebar, the
 * search modal and the chat: the sidebar's amber sentence was removed on 2026-08-21 and the other
 * two never had one. The marks are load bearing, not decoration. The roadmap is the exception and
 * deliberately so — its board keeps an amber notice, because there a filter removes ROWS from a
 * list the reader is counting, and an absence in a list of requests is a conclusion about us.
 *
 * THE WHOLE ROW IS THE TARGET, and until 2026-08-21 it demonstrably was not. `<label>` wraps the
 * input, so the markup was always right; what broke it was this component's own `focusout` guard.
 * A mousedown on the name or the glyph moves focus off the focused checkbox to `<body>`, because a
 * label is not focusable, and the guard read that null `relatedTarget` as "the reader tabbed away"
 * and closed the list between mousedown and mouseup. The click then landed on whatever the popover
 * had been covering. Only the checkbox itself worked, because it is the one focusable thing in the
 * row. The fix is in `lib/platform-picker.ts`: a mousedown on anything in the list that is not the
 * input has its default prevented, so focus never leaves and the label forwards its click normally.
 * That is also what stops a drag across the names selecting text inside a menu, and it is what lets
 * `All` and `None` act without the list closing under the pointer that pressed them.
 *
 * THE MARKS ARE THE RECOGNITION, and they are the one sanctioned exception to Lucide-only. They
 * come from `PlatformMark.astro` and nowhere else. An OFF mark is DESATURATED and never faded away:
 * at 0.55 opacity the ClickUp mark — two gradients that grayscale to a mid tone — composited into
 * the dark ground and vanished outright, which reads as a rendering fault rather than an off state.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY IS THE BUILD, NOT A PASS AFTERWARDS. The control is a disclosure over real
 * `<input type="checkbox">`, so checked state, keyboard operation and the announcement of both come
 * from the platform instead of from us. The trigger carries `aria-expanded` and an `aria-label`
 * that names what is currently chosen, so a screen-reader user gets the state the sighted reader
 * gets from the marks.
 *
 * THE STYLES LIVE HERE, AND THEY HAVE TO. An Astro scoped `<style>` in a PARENT never reaches a
 * child component's markup, and this component is exactly that extraction, so every rule the
 * control needs is in this file. The one form that looks safe and is not is `.parent > *`: Astro
 * appends the OWNING file's scope hash to the trailing `*`, the child carries its own, and the
 * selector ships as valid CSS that matches zero elements. Where a rule has to reach a child's
 * element (the brand marks) it says `:global()` on that half deliberately.
 */
import { PLATFORMS } from '../lib/platforms';
import PlatformMark from './PlatformMark.astro';
/* DOCUMENTED SUBSET, NOT THE IDENTITY CATALOGUE — see `lib/documented-platforms.ts`. A row for a
   platform with no pages is a filter that hides everything and offers nothing back. */
import { DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS } from '../lib/documented-platforms';
import { nextPickerToken } from '../lib/platform-picker';

interface Props {
  /**
   * THE RUNG THIS CONTROL SITS ON, and it is per-surface because the neighbours differ.
   *
   * `sm` (default) is the documentation rung: 6px/12px padding on a 13px face with a 36px floor,
   * which is the size of the things it stands among in a sidebar, a dialog row and a chat rail.
   * `md` is the roadmap rung. Measured on the live board at 1440: a status chip is 36.8px and
   * `Add a request` is 40.5px, and a control asking the question that governs every count below it
   * should not be the shortest thing in the bar. `md` keeps `.rb-chip`'s padding and type exactly —
   * so the two rows read as one system — and raises only the FLOOR to 40px, which puts the trigger
   * one rung above the chips with the primary action still loudest by colour and weight rather than
   * by height.
   *
   * Sized per surface rather than globally BECAUSE THE DOCS ROWS WERE NOT WRONG — Oleh, 2026-08-21:
   * the rest of that design is the part he likes. A global bump would have fixed one row by breaking
   * the one that was already right.
   */
  size?: 'sm' | 'md';
  /**
   * WHERE THE ANSWER LIVES. `shared` is the one stored preference every documentation surface reads,
   * so a choice made in the sidebar is the same choice in search and in the chat. `local` keeps the
   * set on this element and announces it with `pk:change`; nothing else on the site moves.
   *
   * THE ROADMAP IS `local`, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE OLD ONE. `board.ts` used to argue page-local
   * because its facet hid untagged rows and the docs filter must not; adopting the multi-select
   * dissolved that. What replaces it is a VOCABULARY mismatch. This board draws all five platform
   * IDENTITIES, and the documentation surfaces draw only the platforms that have pages. Writing a
   * board choice into the shared preference would let one click on Smartsheet — a platform with no
   * documentation tree — set a preference whose row does not exist in the sidebar's own picker, so
   * the reader could reach a state they had no control left to leave.
   */
  state?: 'shared' | 'local';
  /**
   * WHICH PLATFORMS GET A ROW. `documented` is the subset with a documentation tree and is right
   * for anything that filters PAGES: a row for a platform we have written nothing about is a
   * filter that hides everything and offers nothing back. `all` is the identity catalogue, and the
   * roadmap needs it because there a platform is the SUBJECT of a request rather than a promise of
   * support — "back up Smartsheet" is the most votable row on the board precisely because it is not
   * built yet.
   */
  catalogue?: 'documented' | 'all';
  /**
   * The accessible name for the control. The surfaces keep drawing their own visible labels — the
   * sidebar's "Show docs for" heading, the modal's "Search in" — because that chrome is theirs and
   * only the control is shared. This is what a screen reader hears in their place.
   */
  label: string;
  /**
   * Which way the list opens. The chat's picker sits just above the composer at the foot of a
   * full-height drawer, so a downward list would run off the bottom of the viewport and take its
   * last options with it.
   */
  drop?: 'down' | 'up';
  class?: string;
}

const {
  label,
  state = 'shared',
  catalogue = 'documented',
  drop = 'down',
  class: cls,
  size = 'sm',
} = Astro.props;

/** The rows this instance draws. See the `catalogue` prop for why the two lists differ. */
const ITEMS = catalogue === 'all' ? PLATFORMS : DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS;

/* The token has to come from a real module: an `.astro` frontmatter block re-runs for every
   instance, so a counter declared beside this line would hand out `1` forever. Same reason
   `nextMarkToken` lives in `lib/platforms.ts`. */
const popId = `pk${nextPickerToken()}-list`;

/* The server-rendered state is "everything on", which is what a reader who has never chosen gets
   and what the markup must say before any script runs. A stored preference corrects it at
   hydration; rendering the stored value is not an option on a statically built site.

   EVERYTHING ON AND NOTHING ON ARE THE SAME VIEW, so this first paint is never WRONG about what is
   filtered — at worst it is wrong about where a returning reader left their ticks, and only for the
   frame before the controller runs. */
const restLabel = 'All platforms';
---

<div
  data-pk-size={size}
  class:list={['pk', cls]}
  data-platform-picker
  data-pk-state={state}
  data-pk-drop={drop}
  data-pk-label={label}
>
  <div class="pk-dis">
    <button
      type="button"
      class="pk-trigger"
      data-pk-trigger
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-haspopup="true"
      aria-controls={popId}
      aria-label={`${label}: ${restLabel.toLowerCase()}`}
    >
      <span class="pk-marks" data-pk-marks aria-hidden="true">
        {ITEMS.map((p) => (
          <span class="pk-mark" data-pk-mark={p.id} hidden>
            <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={14} />
          </span>
        ))}
      </span>
      <span class="pk-state" data-pk-state>
        {restLabel}
      </span>
      <svg
        class="pk-caret"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        width="14"
        height="14"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" />
      </svg>
    </button>

    <div class="pk-pop" id={popId} data-pk-pop hidden>
      {/* BOTH ENDS, ONE CLICK, which is the flexibility Oleh named. They are buttons rather than a
          third and fourth checkbox because they are not platforms: they SET the platforms. They sit
          above the list so the reader meets "all or none" before the rows they would otherwise
          tick one at a time, and each is disabled-by-appearance only through its own repaint —
          `All` while everything is on, `None` while nothing is. */}
      <div class="pk-bulk">
        <button type="button" class="pk-bulk-btn" data-pk-all>All</button>
        <button type="button" class="pk-bulk-btn" data-pk-none>None</button>
      </div>

      <div class="pk-opts" role="group" aria-label={label}>
        {ITEMS.map((p) => (
          <label class="pk-opt">
            <input type="checkbox" value={p.id} data-pk-opt={p.id} checked />
            <span class="pk-opt-glyph" aria-hidden="true">
              <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={14} />
            </span>
            <span class="pk-opt-name">{p.name}</span>
          </label>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import { mountPlatformPickers } from '../lib/platform-picker';
  mountPlatformPickers();
</script>

<style>
  /* Starlight's prose rhythm is a GENERAL-SIBLING rule and it reaches inside any flex or grid box
     it can see: first child keeps `margin-top: 0`, every sibling after it takes 16px, and a row of
     controls turns into a staircase. Every flex container here resets its own children. The reset
     uses `:global(*)` on the child half on purpose — a bare `> *` would have Astro's scope hash
     appended to it, and the brand marks come from `PlatformMark.astro` and carry no hash of ours,
     so the rule would ship as valid CSS matching nothing. */
  .pk > :global(*),
  .pk-dis > :global(*),
  .pk-trigger > :global(*),
  .pk-marks > :global(*),
  .pk-bulk > :global(*),
  .pk-opt > :global(*),
  .pk-opts > :global(*) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .pk {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* ── The control ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `display: flex` AND NOT THE BLOCK THIS WAS. A block wraps its inline-flex trigger in a LINE
     box, and a line box is sized by the strut of the containing block's own font plus wherever the
     trigger's baseline happens to fall. The trigger's baseline moves when the brand marks appear,
     so the box around it measured 33.4px at rest and 28px the moment the filter narrowed: a 5.4px
     step in the docs rail every time the reader changed the filter, from a control that had not
     changed size. The same fault as `7bfc27f` in the product, and the same fix. Flex has no line
     boxes, so the box is the trigger's own height in every state. The list is absolutely positioned
     and out of flow, so it is not a flex item and nothing here reaches it. */
  .pk-dis {
    display: flex;
    position: relative;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* THE TRIGGER IS A CONTROL, AND IT WAS DRAWN LIKE A FOOTNOTE. Oleh, 2026-08-21: a narrow thin
     little button rather than something the size of the elements around it. Measured from the
     source it was: 4px + 4px padding, a 1px border either side, and a 12px face at line-height 1.4
     — a 16.8px line box inside a 26.8px box. Everything it stands beside is a third taller.

     WHAT IT IS NOW: 6px/12px padding, a 13px face, a 14px mark, and `min-height: 36px`. That is the
     roadmap status chips' padding and type exactly, one rung under the primary button.

     THE `min-height` IS ALSO A CORRECTNESS FIX, not only a size. The content of this box is an
     18.2px line box beside a 14px mark, so 6 + 18.2 + 6 + 2 = 32.2px of intrinsic height in every
     state — under the floor, which means the floor is what is measured and the box is its declared
     height whether marks are showing or not. The 5.4px step the `display: flex` note above
     describes cannot recur through any other route either. It is also what makes `size` a one-line
     change: the floor is the only thing that has to move.

     EVERY VALUE IS ON THE 4px GRID except the two 1px borders, which is what borders are allowed. */
  .pk-trigger {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    max-width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 36px;
    padding: 6px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      background-color 150ms ease-out,
      border-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  /* THE ROADMAP RUNG, AND ONLY THE FLOOR MOVES. `.rb-chip` is `6px 12px` on a 13px face with a 14px
     glyph and measures 36.8px; the base rule above is already that padding and that type, so
     matching it takes no declaration at all. What `md` buys is four pixels of box: 40px puts this
     just above the chip rung and level with `Add a request` (40.5px), which is where the control
     that governs every count on the board belongs. The action stays loudest because it is filled
     and this is not — height was never what made it the primary. */
  [data-pk-size='md'] .pk-trigger {
    min-height: 40px;
  }

  .pk-trigger:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .pk-trigger:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  /* Open reads as raised: the ground lifts and the border takes the accent. */
  .pk-trigger[aria-expanded='true'] {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
  }

  .pk-marks {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    flex: none;
  }

  .pk-mark {
    display: flex;
  }

  .pk-mark[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .pk-state {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .pk-caret {
    flex: none;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    transition: transform 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .pk-trigger[aria-expanded='true'] .pk-caret {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pk-caret,
    .pk-trigger {
      transition-duration: 0s;
    }
  }

  /* ── The list ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `position: absolute` and NOT `fixed`, deliberately. Fixed would escape the docs sidebar's
     scrolling pane, but the chat drawer animates with `transform: translateX(…)` and a transformed
     ancestor becomes the containing block for a fixed descendant, so the viewport coordinates
     would silently be measured against the wrong box. Absolute has one requirement instead — that
     no ancestor clips it — and all three surfaces satisfy it: the sidebar pane scrolls rather than
     clips, the chat drawer sets no overflow, and the search dialog's `overflow: hidden` is 512px
     tall with this row near its top.

     NO SHADOW. There is no elevation token in the bridge and a shadow mixed from `--bo-base-content`
     would be black in light and WHITE in dark, which is worse than none. An opaque ground and a
     hairline is what the rest of this portal uses to say "above". */
  .pk-pop {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 20;
    inset-block-start: calc(100% + 4px);
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    min-width: 11rem;
    width: max-content;
    max-width: min(100%, 16rem);
    padding: 4px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
  }

  .pk[data-pk-drop='up'] .pk-pop {
    inset-block-start: auto;
    inset-block-end: calc(100% + 4px);
  }

  .pk-pop[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* BOTH ENDS OF THE RANGE, SET APART FROM THE ROWS THEY SET. A hairline under them rather than a
     heading: they are two words, and a label over two words is more chrome than content. They read
     one rung quieter than the platform rows because they are the less common act — 12px is the
     floor and they sit on it. */
  .pk-bulk {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 0 4px 4px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .pk-bulk-btn {
    padding: 4px 8px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    background: none;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-12);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      background-color 150ms ease-out,
      color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .pk-bulk-btn:hover {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .pk-bulk-btn:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  /* THE END YOU ARE ALREADY AT IS NOT AN ACTION. It keeps `aria-disabled` rather than `disabled`,
     so it stays in the tab order and stays announced — a control that vanishes from the keyboard
     because of what is ticked elsewhere is harder to understand than one that says it has nothing
     to do — and it is drawn inert so a pointer is not invited to press it. */
  .pk-bulk-btn[aria-disabled='true'] {
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    opacity: 0.55;
    cursor: default;
  }

  .pk-bulk-btn[aria-disabled='true']:hover {
    background: none;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .pk-opts {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
  }

  .pk-opt {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    padding: 6px 8px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.4;
    cursor: pointer;
    /* The padding is part of the target, so the highlight has to reach it. Selecting the words
       inside a menu row is never what the pointer meant. */
    user-select: none;
    transition: background-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  /* THE ROW IS THE TARGET AND IT HAS TO LOOK LIKE ONE. The hover was `--bo-base-200`, which is the
     product's interaction grey — but this portal overrides that token to `#f4f5f7` in light so the
     docs floor could be white, and #f4f5f7 on the popover's #ffffff is a three-percent step that a
     reader does not see. The soft-primary pair is the portal's own "this one" paint, it is solved
     separately in each theme so it does not fade out on white, and it is two documented rungs of
     one recipe rather than a new colour: 20% for hover, 26% for the press. */
  .pk-opt:hover {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
  }

  .pk-opt:active {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg-hover);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pk-opt,
    .pk-bulk-btn {
      transition-duration: 0s;
    }
  }

  /* The native control keeps its own shape, its own keyboard behaviour and its own announcement.
     `accent-color` is the whole of the theming a checkbox needs, and reinventing one for flavour is
     a standard affordance broken for nothing. */
  .pk-opt input {
    flex: none;
    margin: 0;
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    accent-color: var(--bo-primary);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .pk-opt:has(input:focus-visible) {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  .pk-opt-glyph {
    display: flex;
    flex: none;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* OFF IS DESATURATED, NEVER FADED AWAY. A list where one logo disappears reads as a rendering
     fault, and the silhouette is what makes the rows scannable without reading them. 0.8 and not
     the 0.55 the chips shipped with: at 0.55 the ClickUp mark composited into the dark ground and
     vanished entirely, which is the exact failure this rule exists to prevent. Measured, not
     guessed. */
  .pk-opt:not(:has(input:checked)) .pk-opt-glyph :global(.bo-mark) {
    filter: grayscale(1);
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .pk-opt-name {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
